<p>
The other day, <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13722">I wrote about repeat mode</a>. My take was that it was a way repeating certain commands without having to retype their, possibly, complex prefixes. All of that is true but as <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13722#comment-6859794787">Karthik informed me in a comment</a>, there is much, much more to repeat mode than simply repeating commands.
</p>
<p>
It is a way, he says, of grouping a set of related commands together into a sort of mode. Thus, there is more to <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <kbd>}</kbd> than simply repeating the enlarge window command. Once you type <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> you can type any of <code>{</code>, <code>}</code>, <code>^</code>, <code>v</code> to resize the window in any direction. The <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> enables a keymap with those four single keys to resize the current window, defining, in effect, a &ldquo;resize window mode&rdquo;.
</p>
<p>
Four years ago, Karthik wrote <a href="https://karthinks.com/software/it-bears-repeating/">a long post</a> that explains all this and, at least on an intuitive level, how it works. My first thought was to add an update to my post that pointed to Karthik&rsquo;s and I did that but then I thought that his post was so good that I should devote a new post to it so that anyone who missed it the first time would see it.
</p>
<p>
Repeat mode really is an excellent facility—Karthik says it&rsquo;s a cornerstone of his Emacs usage—and every Emacser should be familiar with it. If nothing else, it&rsquo;s worth enabling repeat mode so that you can use the built in repeat maps. You can see what they are by running the command <code>describe-repeat-maps</code>.</p>
